Z||000000||sqoach||02-16-2001||04:09 PM||||i did a search and didn't find any info on replacing the alternator in the 4cyl. found a little on a v6. i also looked in my repair manual, and i can't really figure it out. i'm not all that good following book instructions sometimes.<P>what am i gonna have to remove? it looks like the dogbone may have to come out. i don't want to screw anything up, so some suggestions will be greatly appreciated. <P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG">  <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 02-16-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||02-16-2001||04:27 PM||||<BR>The last time I did the alternator on my 84 Duck I dropped in out the bottom. It seemed to work ok, just have to manuver the alt around a bit to get it out.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||02-16-2001||08:57 PM||||it looks like all the bolts are kinda hard to get to without removing something. you didn't have to take anything else out or move something?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||02-16-2001||10:39 PM||||<BR>I just went to look at the setup - to refresh my memory.  On my 84, here is what I would do:<P>1) disconnect the negative battery ground cable<P>2) remove the wire leading to the back of the alternator ( I don't know the name of the wire, but it is the one with the rubber gromet)  requires a 10 mm wrench/ratchet-socket<P>3) remove the top bolt, should be a 13mm<P>4) remove the bottom bolt, also 13mm (I think)<P>5) unplug the connector, then drop the alternator through the bottom.<P><BR>Note: depending on the oil filter used, you may have to remove the filter to reach the bottom bolt.<P>Also, you should be able to reach all the bolts through the top, but you may need extensions.<P>From what I see, bottom removal is easiest and nothing will need to be removed, except maybe the oil filter.<P>Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-17-2001||12:22 AM||||You need the wheel blocked, the parking brake on if it works, and the car out of gear. Then you can undo the strut and shove the motor forward to gain room. If the tranny has 2 mounts you won't get much more room but every little bit helps.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||02-17-2001||12:26 AM||||thanks for the steps 86se. i was looking at the oil filter and i might have to take it out. but, if i don't feel like getting oily, i'll try theogre's way. thanks for the info guys.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gold-86SE||02-17-2001||01:41 AM||||<BR>Just as an added thought: let us know how you did it.  The question may pop up again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bgoo27||02-17-2001||12:04 PM||||I too am in the process of replacing my 87 Fiero alternator and I have it disconnected, but can't find a way to get it out the bottom of the engine compartment past the right wheel toe link rod.  I have tried to remove the toe link rod, but the bolt seems frozen in place.  Does anyone have any suggestions for a novice mechanic?||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||02-17-2001||06:41 PM||||I have taken my alternator out from the top on my 84 2.5.<P>I done this when i had to replace the oil pan gasket.  You will probably need to use a U joint plus various extensions.  <P>You can remove the aircleaner, remove the bolt holding the alternator on top.  Remove that black thing that holds the alternator.  This is the only thing that requires the U joint and it does become tricky.  After that you can pull the long bolt thats on the bottom of the alternator.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||02-18-2001||12:19 AM||||bgoo... <P>It wont come out "the bottom" on any 4 cyl that I can tell.<P>To do my 87.....<P>I did what I said above.<P>Then pulled the alt out between the intake, trunk wall, and tranny. sort of out the side you might say. was a pain in the ass.<P>I have an 87 automatic so I get more room than a stick or an older auto would.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Motorhead||02-18-2001||04:12 AM||||Pulled the alternator off the '84 last weekend.  Ogres' method is right on.  Bottom bolt came out mostly by feel and odd looking contortions.  Be sure to use some penetrating oil on the top bolt if it looks the least bit coroded.  Mine broke off in the alternator body.  <P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10234998&p=38686771&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||02-18-2001||05:46 PM||||I got the bottom bolt out with a couple feet of extension and a universal. Makes it pretty painless when you can turn the wrench in the clear over the tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000012||sqoach||02-19-2001||06:54 PM||||alrighty. it wasn't too hard. just took a little time. i decided on taking it out the top. i supported the engine with a floor jack, and took off the motor-side of the dog bone strut. i found out my strut needed replacing. i then took out the alternator bracket, and then i could easily get to the alternator with no problems. after the alternator and bracket are out of the way, it's easy to get to the trunk-side of the dog bone strut and take it out.<P>i do have a question about the alternator. how many amps is a stock one suppose to put out? and how many volts?<BR>i may sell my "old" alternator if someone needs it. it doesn't really look too bad. i just got one that put out more amps.||||reg||1
Z||000013||qwikgta||02-19-2001||08:45 PM||||I wasn't going to post but I couldn't believe that after all the advice and even a full on discription in the book you still went in from the top,  It took me 15 min to change this from the bottom, like the book says. You removed the bone, unbolted the motor mount, its three bolts and off.  Sorry for being a jerk but sometimes we make things to difficult.  <P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>QWIKGTA<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000014||sqoach||02-19-2001||11:06 PM||||there were a couple of reasons i took it out the top. first, i had to check the dog bone strut because it looked a little worn. when i got it out, i noticed it was worse than i thought. second, it looked like i was going to have to take my oil filter off to bring it out the bottom. i didn't feel like getting oil everywhere, so i didn't mess with it. if these two things were not an issue, i would have most likely removed it from the bottom.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierospeeder||02-20-2001||02:17 AM||||I know doing it from the top might be more things to pull off but i find it easier to do it then going underneath.<P>For me its a hassle getting the jack and putting the car up, and the fiero is a car low to the ground, which makes it less comfortable working underneath.  Plus i dont own a creeper yet after 4 years, since ive been working on cars.  ||||reg||1
